  'General Physics',
  'This course teaches introductory physics through the lens of human movement, exercise, sport, rehabilitation, and health-science instrumentation. Students develop fluency with mechanics, fluids, thermal physics, waves, optics, electricity, magnetism, and modern physics while repeatedly applying those ideas to bodies, equipment, environments, and data. The goal is not only to solve physics problems, but to reason like a movement scientist: define the system, model the forces and motion, measure carefully, communicate uncertainty, and translate findings into defensible performance or clinical insight.',
  $gpjson${"slug": "general-physics", "title": "General Physics", "description": "This course teaches introductory physics through the lens of human movement, exercise, sport, rehabilitation, and health-science instrumentation. Students develop fluency with mechanics, fluids, thermal physics, waves, optics, electricity, magnetism, and modern physics while repeatedly applying those ideas to bodies, equipment, environments, and data. The goal is not only to solve physics problems, but to reason like a movement scientist: define the system, model the forces and motion, measure carefully, communicate uncertainty, and translate findings into defensible performance or clinical insight.", "topics": [{"id": "6946e641-6b99-49e4-b598-d0da264a8b7a", "type": "topic", "title": "Measurement, Units, Modeling, and Quantitative Reasoning for Movement Science", "description": "This opening topic builds the habits that make physics usable in kinesiology: careful measurement, defensible assumptions, dimensional checks, and model-based reasoning about the moving body.", "children": [{"id": "32350b91-2b51-42a7-8be6-f6caf977d8c7", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Measurement systems and dimensional analysis", "description": "Students learn to treat units as a language of physical meaning, catching errors before they become bad conclusions.", "children": [{"id": "4504d833-c113-4936-8352-06ac48ce3b40", "type": "concept", "title": "SI and common clinical/sport units"}, {"id": "2307ebfe-15dc-4014-a7f8-52722771308c", "type": "concept", "title": "Dimensional consistency and unit conversion"}, {"id": "45fee0c2-6484-4c40-8e0f-3269835735c6", "type": "concept", "title": "Order-of-magnitude estimation"}]}, {"id": "45cf3599-79ea-42b7-8293-258af14c0d14", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Modeling the body and equipment", "description": "The body is complex, so students practice choosing simplified models that are useful without pretending they are perfect.", "children": [{"id": "c20d3c22-eaa6-4699-9250-280a62abd2af", "type": "concept", "title": "Particle, rigid-body, and linked-segment models"}, {"id": "e4695ce1-32d5-41b3-bace-f40392772351", "type": "concept", "title": "Assumptions, constraints, and model limitations"}, {"id": "df71aae3-dd6b-43bc-a67b-8f780efe31ca", "type": "concept", "title": "Scaling laws for size, mass, and performance"}]}, {"id": "ed18df45-fca7-4c72-bb04-8de748361a7c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Uncertainty and significant figures", "description": "Physics in kinesiology depends on data quality; this subtopic teaches students to express confidence and limits honestly.", "children": [{"id": "1dfff735-9d89-4fae-846c-e9278d5f3882", "type": "concept", "title": "Random vs. systematic error"}, {"id": "e2ab4c32-c0b5-4256-b94d-23d585e4f731", "type": "concept", "title": "Precision, accuracy, and resolution"}, {"id": "0b1a286e-e2f5-44b2-9dbb-d9d1f7541bed", "type": "concept", "title": "Propagation of uncertainty in calculated results"}]}]}, {"id": "192584b4-89ed-4858-ba6a-4fbc45f69dcd", "type": "topic", "title": "Vectors, Scalars, Free-Body Diagrams, and Coordinate Systems", "description": "Movement science depends on direction as much as magnitude. This topic gives students the visual and mathematical grammar for representing forces, motion, and body orientation.", "children": [{"id": "59f5d40c-1562-4c39-b7e1-50809ed38fa5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Vectors and components", "description": "Students learn to decompose motion and force into usable pieces so complex movement becomes solvable.", "children": [{"id": "db4130ff-aff9-4f16-9961-097b14493e3c", "type": "concept", "title": "Scalar-vector distinction"}, {"id": "5a795337-7a4a-460d-87e6-dae933077be5", "type": "concept", "title": "Cartesian components and resultants"}, {"id": "be805e43-18aa-47c6-9afd-09af7e084ce5", "type": "concept", "title": "Unit vectors and anatomical directions"}]}, {"id": "7ed20368-3773-49ee-accd-657c46165590", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Free-body diagrams", "description": "Free-body diagrams help students identify exactly which interactions matter in a movement or exercise problem.", "children": [{"id": "9cd30a8b-293b-4e6d-b525-b79ecfef1c66", "type": "concept", "title": "Body isolation and external forces"}, {"id": "e35479aa-adf3-4089-ab69-9ec0bd115223", "type": "concept", "title": "Normal, friction, tension, weight, and applied forces"}, {"id": "388c1012-5fd3-4abe-974c-25a8029aebb3", "type": "concept", "title": "Common diagram mistakes in movement settings"}]}, {"id": "7d893be7-7d25-4d27-a55a-10e5baf64d07", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Reference frames and coordinate systems", "description": "Students learn that conclusions depend on the frame of observation, especially in sport, gait, and equipment analysis.", "children": [{"id": "cd8741b4-07be-408c-abcb-3504bdaa7210", "type": "concept", "title": "Laboratory vs. body-centered coordinates"}, {"id": "4b6b3cc9-7c71-4c71-9b75-b1da1292541e", "type": "concept", "title": "Sagittal, frontal, and transverse-plane conventions"}, {"id": "494245a5-1e00-4868-89f1-4de8322ba69b", "type": "concept", "title": "Relative and absolute motion"}]}]}, {"id": "d792dfa1-42a1-4adf-99ce-7ad838155212", "type": "topic", "title": "Linear Kinematics of Human Motion", "description": "This topic analyzes motion without first asking why it happens. It prepares students to describe gait, jumping, throwing, and rehabilitation tasks using displacement, velocity, and acceleration.", "children": [{"id": "a4cca952-c334-4481-8228-d46e2b4068d8", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Position, displacement, velocity, and acceleration", "description": "Students learn how to translate observed motion into quantitative descriptions.", "children": [{"id": "5cadb21e-9c8f-446f-835f-71a8f9719a25", "type": "concept", "title": "One- and two-dimensional motion"}, {"id": "d260bb2d-28c1-4ed8-ac0b-8d22820968ed", "type": "concept", "title": "Average vs. instantaneous quantities"}, {"id": "7acb0d02-f8c2-43a2-a98d-423584bb663c", "type": "concept", "title": "Graphs of position, velocity, and acceleration"}]}, {"id": "0fb4db4b-a349-4150-a0e5-297d3e2708cc", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Projectile motion in sport and exercise", "description": "Projectile motion links physics to jumping, throwing, striking, and landing tasks.", "children": [{"id": "be78321b-eee0-49dd-8618-000122e85201", "type": "concept", "title": "Launch angle and launch speed"}, {"id": "10d3761c-0d64-4604-b19d-1e6980f00d73", "type": "concept", "title": "Trajectory and range"}, {"id": "83ab5a20-ec4b-417d-aaea-7d02f3b765c4", "type": "concept", "title": "Air resistance as a modeling limitation"}]}, {"id": "e7a96dac-7464-46b5-a623-dc365bbb0255", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Video-based movement analysis", "description": "Students connect physics equations to practical tools used in coaching, clinical assessment, and research labs.", "children": [{"id": "a9e04d46-c97d-4632-8a10-ae9bb8fbe664", "type": "concept", "title": "Frame rate and sampling"}, {"id": "4b042f6a-46d4-4965-a8b5-0b71c93f92cf", "type": "concept", "title": "Digitizing landmarks"}, {"id": "ad5bdec5-4665-468a-91df-c473b860b065", "type": "concept", "title": "Calculating velocity and acceleration from position data"}]}]}, {"id": "555bd80f-2056-424b-b27a-9db277b88b1a", "type": "topic", "title": "Newton's Laws, Forces, and Interaction Models", "description": "Dynamics explains why motion changes. Students use Newton's laws to connect external loads, ground reaction forces, friction, and body acceleration.", "children": [{"id": "84ddf680-800c-4401-a7e9-03211a4d8a62", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Newton's three laws in movement contexts", "description": "Students learn to interpret movement as the result of interactions rather than isolated effort.", "children": [{"id": "3c7515ca-efa7-4b1e-98fe-d10c70aa2b2f", "type": "concept", "title": "Inertia and motion state"}, {"id": "b64e69a1-2f28-4143-bca2-236e39f5f41d", "type": "concept", "title": "Net force and acceleration"}, {"id": "8f504dfa-cc96-4d0f-aa26-2540f83b4681", "type": "concept", "title": "Action-reaction pairs"}]}, {"id": "88b3c142-65f0-4108-9652-b4f90a433feb", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Common forces in kinesiology", "description": "Students identify forces that shape human performance and safety in exercise environments.", "children": [{"id": "19cd5153-a520-45ef-b466-06fb2cec4ce0", "type": "concept", "title": "Weight, normal force, friction, tension, and drag"}, {"id": "783bc713-08c0-4a0a-b16c-ff4d1c7caddf", "type": "concept", "title": "Ground reaction forces"}, {"id": "e1d0d728-b136-4c5e-bd82-4da8e7e853a8", "type": "concept", "title": "Contact forces in equipment and surfaces"}]}, {"id": "d2843e90-b878-4d9c-889d-3eb9dd405b57", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Friction, traction, and stability", "description": "Friction determines whether the body can accelerate, stop, turn, or maintain balance.", "children": [{"id": "89f2a397-2471-4ef1-813d-65b14eec5b64", "type": "concept", "title": "Static and kinetic friction"}, {"id": "6d680119-f590-464d-8f67-26554b3f3eaa", "type": "concept", "title": "Coefficient of friction"}, {"id": "0a86358b-7a5d-486e-bb15-952909a7b9dc", "type": "concept", "title": "Footwear, flooring, and injury prevention"}]}]}, {"id": "bdfa3415-471d-4b0e-aa63-5a1398276971", "type": "topic", "title": "Work, Energy, Power, and Metabolic Connections", "description": "Energy methods provide a powerful way to analyze movement, exercise output, and mechanical efficiency without solving every force at every instant.", "children": [{"id": "551fec33-3c0c-40fa-8727-204e24ef57dd", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Mechanical work and kinetic energy", "description": "Students learn how force over distance changes the body's motion.", "children": [{"id": "8c9c1c47-a68c-45af-9ba1-e7db7289fcbe", "type": "concept", "title": "Work by constant and variable forces"}, {"id": "cf0a546c-7794-44b7-a19f-a67ecd80bae0", "type": "concept", "title": "Kinetic energy and the work-energy theorem"}, {"id": "19131f9a-c95f-41ea-97e8-6f32dbe6053f", "type": "concept", "title": "Positive and negative work in movement"}]}, {"id": "32cd04a2-07de-4020-8868-f60e097fa9da", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Potential energy and energy conservation", "description": "Students connect body position, storage, release, and transformation of energy.", "children": [{"id": "af0cd57a-c629-482d-a7d5-31f311f89ece", "type": "concept", "title": "Gravitational potential energy"}, {"id": "47989d05-1dac-46e4-9112-a87570e82829", "type": "concept", "title": "Elastic energy in tendons and equipment"}, {"id": "04f10ef7-aa34-49e7-961f-281718175e81", "type": "concept", "title": "Conservative and nonconservative forces"}]}, {"id": "6a18ab95-b340-4b2a-b29a-be425375a22d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Power, efficiency, and human performance", "description": "Students connect physics to exercise physiology through the rate of energy transfer.", "children": [{"id": "ebf421a6-4b39-4ce7-a455-abe717e797f8", "type": "concept", "title": "Mechanical power output"}, {"id": "f97a0a67-54e8-4a18-9dbc-de15ee4a4886", "type": "concept", "title": "Efficiency and energy loss"}, {"id": "3943f6de-9098-452f-a7ab-f2fb882fa9d5", "type": "concept", "title": "Cycling, running, lifting, and jumping examples"}]}]}, {"id": "fb93ed43-3675-470b-912e-3fecaf3814d7", "type": "topic", "title": "Momentum, Impulse, Collisions, and Impact Safety", "description": "Momentum and impulse help students analyze rapid force events such as landing, cutting, collisions, falls, and sport impacts.", "children": [{"id": "92551632-0295-4384-ae68-73ec8cadb92a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Linear momentum and conservation", "description": "Students learn when momentum is conserved and how it constrains motion before and after interactions.", "children": [{"id": "a02dd5a4-8bfe-47d9-bc6a-b1aa3b3f4c64", "type": "concept", "title": "Momentum as mass times velocity"}, {"id": "20e1f828-925f-46d1-a336-f494e1269565", "type": "concept", "title": "System boundaries"}, {"id": "13d99e44-a13c-4cb2-8b5a-97501a30b32b", "type": "concept", "title": "Conservation in low-external-force intervals"}]}, {"id": "b78bfe27-00e7-4b48-b1de-f8503d299685", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Impulse and force-time relationships", "description": "Impulse shows why both force magnitude and contact time matter for performance and safety.", "children": [{"id": "0bc0f6c8-ba68-411a-b677-f41bea88bbb2", "type": "concept", "title": "Impulse-momentum theorem"}, {"id": "aaddb78a-f9b7-4953-a5d5-fbf58f905c44", "type": "concept", "title": "Force-time curves"}, {"id": "4ed85b1e-a3fb-4e43-b9c1-5e4258c72474", "type": "concept", "title": "Landing technique and load attenuation"}]}, {"id": "0f89939d-dd99-465e-928e-f7699dfbe703", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Collisions and impact mechanics", "description": "Students distinguish productive impact from dangerous loading by examining energy transfer and deformation.", "children": [{"id": "fa7108e4-02a6-4ac8-9a3b-9c93082474fd", "type": "concept", "title": "Elastic and inelastic collisions"}, {"id": "5119ac21-4c63-4c12-bb2c-cdda3357c8f3", "type": "concept", "title": "Coefficient of restitution"}, {"id": "e06e7625-7d92-44d9-8966-67a97d286ac5", "type": "concept", "title": "Helmets, mats, footwear, and protective design"}]}]}, {"id": "f6c2a304-d74e-47ca-b32d-dee7b12bba98", "type": "topic", "title": "Torque, Rotational Motion, and Angular Momentum", "description": "Most human movement is rotational around joints. This topic gives students the tools to analyze turning, swinging, joint moments, and angular control.", "children": [{"id": "2a033e9f-ddaa-4b57-a1f0-6c17bca37e38", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Angular kinematics", "description": "Students learn rotational analogs of linear motion so they can describe joint and segment motion.", "children": [{"id": "946d01e4-dcc3-4827-9529-184ecba7cdf0", "type": "concept", "title": "Angular displacement, velocity, and acceleration"}, {"id": "d21dca3f-99e6-4a57-bbff-2e55f279fea4", "type": "concept", "title": "Radians and degrees"}, {"id": "f1c7d2f4-8e2a-4ffe-a604-c4eadb7fdbe1", "type": "concept", "title": "Relationship between linear and angular quantities"}]}, {"id": "f6af1da6-b0a0-4380-8937-e33839c0fc67", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Torque and moment arms", "description": "Torque explains how forces create rotation at joints and equipment pivots.", "children": [{"id": "01b7b836-398d-44f9-aea9-dc1ec765d7e7", "type": "concept", "title": "Torque as force times moment arm"}, {"id": "4c47f969-ebb8-4f04-888c-2e0fe21855e5", "type": "concept", "title": "Internal and external moments"}, {"id": "39b37997-f454-46fa-a9ff-041ad5848445", "type": "concept", "title": "Joint torque examples"}]}, {"id": "080d43ad-5e56-479b-9613-3ff15154830c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Moment of inertia and angular momentum", "description": "Students learn why mass distribution changes rotational difficulty and control.", "children": [{"id": "eacbb222-77be-441f-8763-40b097faea29", "type": "concept", "title": "Moment of inertia"}, {"id": "eb62cfc1-98f2-42ad-af14-b01d27847e28", "type": "concept", "title": "Angular impulse and angular momentum"}, {"id": "c25b2426-33d4-46d8-b7de-4abf81b17276", "type": "concept", "title": "Spin, swing, and aerial movement applications"}]}]}, {"id": "3b889fee-f59c-49a7-b249-b47f0b06aa3e", "type": "topic", "title": "Static Equilibrium, Center of Mass, and Human Balance", "description": "Balance is a physics problem with clinical and performance consequences. Students analyze when bodies and objects remain stable or tip, rotate, or fall.", "children": [{"id": "622b8aa2-3be2-4ff5-8590-fa12b8e72c84", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Conditions for equilibrium", "description": "Students learn that stillness requires both zero net force and zero net torque.", "children": [{"id": "1edbaa0e-3680-4f27-bdb8-ea443b311b6d", "type": "concept", "title": "Translational equilibrium"}, {"id": "d85e786b-7c5a-44ae-bc9f-fbf2f20fa48c", "type": "concept", "title": "Rotational equilibrium"}, {"id": "ef798b9e-5c45-4cce-9147-1473540bee51", "type": "concept", "title": "Solving multi-force equilibrium problems"}]}, {"id": "5fb83843-dcf1-4dbb-bc78-662f7cff0356", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Center of mass and base of support", "description": "Students connect body posture to stability, fall risk, and sport skill execution.", "children": [{"id": "7d4434a3-adb0-480c-abd6-d302117dd70c", "type": "concept", "title": "Center of mass location"}, {"id": "42afc347-25c2-4c22-94bb-77ef5b825121", "type": "concept", "title": "Line of gravity"}, {"id": "3f9d154b-ef0e-4aa9-b02d-949615686071", "type": "concept", "title": "Base of support and stability limits"}]}, {"id": "b6f3d1ab-e34a-472d-a6b9-a6121eb13216", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Balance assessment and applications", "description": "Students apply equilibrium to real movement settings rather than treating it as abstract statics.", "children": [{"id": "6312693a-80e0-4de7-a21a-b348765dcb04", "type": "concept", "title": "Single-leg stance and postural sway"}, {"id": "7659ead7-18a1-4253-a6da-658033278b04", "type": "concept", "title": "Load carriage and assistive devices"}, {"id": "c001c70d-4481-4a6c-93bf-06516c6cea70", "type": "concept", "title": "Older adult and rehabilitation examples"}]}]}, {"id": "dcae89e4-ad88-4e79-855f-2812058f863a", "type": "topic", "title": "Levers, Mechanical Advantage, and Musculoskeletal Applications", "description": "The musculoskeletal system acts through lever arrangements. Students learn how bones, joints, muscles, and external loads create mechanical advantages and tradeoffs.", "children": [{"id": "a4a9a3c7-a54b-46d0-b64e-a00d7f181cc5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Classes of levers in the body", "description": "Students identify how body structures transform muscle force into movement or stability.", "children": [{"id": "71208d84-4a29-4b36-97c6-84afd4373127", "type": "concept", "title": "First-, second-, and third-class levers"}, {"id": "4413e961-f08d-4bee-a871-e20db5f150a2", "type": "concept", "title": "Fulcrum, effort, and load"}, {"id": "f23fbb97-d88c-47ba-9299-adc57cb5712d", "type": "concept", "title": "Examples at neck, ankle, elbow, and knee"}]}, {"id": "340b65c2-bb68-42f2-9b92-580aae50c40f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Mechanical advantage and tradeoffs", "description": "Students learn why the body often favors speed and range of motion over force advantage.", "children": [{"id": "c5d0cd9f-4515-47d1-ac21-4129eb1b2395", "type": "concept", "title": "Mechanical advantage ratio"}, {"id": "ae5af7d5-06de-43b3-9583-325caa028a3e", "type": "concept", "title": "Force-speed-distance tradeoffs"}, {"id": "42d07351-8192-4ab0-8431-26ed83f25431", "type": "concept", "title": "Exercise technique implications"}]}, {"id": "7958b5fb-9898-4c6c-bf26-2aa1257754bf", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Joint loading and resistance exercise", "description": "Students connect lever mechanics to training decisions and injury-aware programming.", "children": [{"id": "ced01c42-4c14-4ea5-aba6-d26118bff731", "type": "concept", "title": "External moment arms in exercises"}, {"id": "98c31d93-8e0c-4ef0-bb65-82bd2319891d", "type": "concept", "title": "Load placement and joint stress"}, {"id": "0bfc4207-ee26-4a04-9cc5-d95e1bc29318", "type": "concept", "title": "Machines, free weights, and bands"}]}]}, {"id": "e8534bbe-f05f-4616-a65d-d265dd67a246", "type": "topic", "title": "Elasticity, Stress, Strain, and Tissue/Equipment Mechanics", "description": "Movement depends on materials: bone, tendon, muscle, footwear, mats, and equipment all deform under load. This topic introduces the physics of deformation and injury mechanisms.", "children": [{"id": "3a74ca8d-492d-4cd0-a7de-83aa50c2ce8d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Stress, strain, and elastic behavior", "description": "Students learn how internal loading is quantified and why deformation matters.", "children": [{"id": "28661205-7923-47af-9c20-0348526aef49", "type": "concept", "title": "Normal and shear stress"}, {"id": "50a1c04a-a697-40d1-bd07-0088d922cc6c", "type": "concept", "title": "Strain and deformation"}, {"id": "469f9696-d5db-44e5-9295-e3f6b9975cfb", "type": "concept", "title": "Young's modulus and stiffness"}]}, {"id": "6c40073d-657b-41f4-8221-337d4ae29ebb", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Biological tissue mechanics", "description": "Students connect material properties to performance, adaptation, and injury risk.", "children": [{"id": "6d2b2fe0-2f99-4e79-9c98-19584461b8be", "type": "concept", "title": "Bone, tendon, ligament, and muscle properties"}, {"id": "fa39b111-a593-4ffc-b312-a76a15f3ac60", "type": "concept", "title": "Viscoelasticity and hysteresis"}, {"id": "dc08942c-cc5e-4df2-9a80-f1c2125147f4", "type": "concept", "title": "Creep, relaxation, and fatigue"}]}, {"id": "a5dc9ad8-6d7f-4899-bdf2-9ea2692db781", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Equipment and surface mechanics", "description": "Students evaluate how materials mediate force transfer between person and environment.", "children": [{"id": "6bc85807-05a1-43e0-9ff8-8baee026e876", "type": "concept", "title": "Shoe cushioning and stiffness"}, {"id": "63dfa154-f842-4f0a-9e08-b0caed17905e", "type": "concept", "title": "Mats and impact attenuation"}, {"id": "55b6dece-aa21-4768-9c7b-5cc68abd2492", "type": "concept", "title": "Prosthetic and orthotic materials"}]}]}, {"id": "875e8d7e-8fde-4768-97c6-b7e9b2d46248", "type": "topic", "title": "Fluids, Pressure, Buoyancy, and Cardiopulmonary Applications", "description": "Fluid physics connects swimming, blood flow, breathing, pressure, buoyancy, and exercise in water or air.", "children": [{"id": "b3091e52-d21b-4f17-bdd1-35ff9bbd3f70", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Pressure and hydrostatics", "description": "Students learn how fluid pressure varies with depth and affects bodies in water and circulation.", "children": [{"id": "c9c24747-ca4f-4524-acad-21572bc37425", "type": "concept", "title": "Pressure definition and units"}, {"id": "656bb696-0a18-4fd6-94a3-ce6cd9e08cf9", "type": "concept", "title": "Pascal's principle"}, {"id": "44a22d41-785d-47df-a50d-0a4a0e1b9b26", "type": "concept", "title": "Hydrostatic pressure in aquatic exercise"}]}, {"id": "aeaaef01-9c6e-44c9-8b4d-92a5d6ca6aca", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Buoyancy and flotation", "description": "Students connect Archimedes' principle to swimming, rehabilitation, and body composition contexts.", "children": [{"id": "dccb0c7f-01b2-4053-b94f-865da08e93bc", "type": "concept", "title": "Buoyant force"}, {"id": "6506d65b-ea7b-43a5-b671-abcb19b0241e", "type": "concept", "title": "Apparent weight in water"}, {"id": "4ffb7bf1-c189-4cd3-9d66-299cdfe49bf0", "type": "concept", "title": "Center of buoyancy and stability"}]}, {"id": "d1f83814-36c6-42e5-a4a3-e788cc05ac88", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Fluid flow, drag, and biological transport", "description": "Students study how flow resistance shapes swimming, cycling, blood flow, and breathing.", "children": [{"id": "25359366-334a-44b0-9b66-9ce8e00df6dc", "type": "concept", "title": "Continuity and flow rate"}, {"id": "6422d192-7d04-4ded-8842-a78aced7c30a", "type": "concept", "title": "Laminar vs. turbulent flow"}, {"id": "449cbec4-6f1e-4d77-96d1-92d74abebda1", "type": "concept", "title": "Drag force and streamlining"}]}]}, {"id": "fdf2db2a-ba07-4f00-ab82-3454f986a609", "type": "topic", "title": "Heat, Thermodynamics, and Exercise Environments", "description": "Thermal physics helps students understand body temperature regulation, environmental stress, heat transfer, and energy transformation during exercise.", "children": [{"id": "9d90da77-ce94-444e-b987-e61f54590a82", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Temperature, heat, and internal energy", "description": "Students distinguish thermal concepts that are often confused in everyday language.", "children": [{"id": "3be23d9c-2f31-4e82-bfe2-d8d646d1a247", "type": "concept", "title": "Temperature vs. heat"}, {"id": "ff562ba0-b622-4ffc-9230-f497ad5014e5", "type": "concept", "title": "Specific heat capacity"}, {"id": "60c2a15d-ee9f-4843-bc72-2dd552326615", "type": "concept", "title": "Internal energy and molecular motion"}]}, {"id": "1d7bb352-2781-46b2-8463-b3b37c2b86ae", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Modes of heat transfer", "description": "Students learn how bodies gain and lose heat in real environments.", "children": [{"id": "2594e6e1-d3a7-4970-a9ff-593bf5800614", "type": "concept", "title": "Conduction, convection, radiation, and evaporation"}, {"id": "d170cafa-42b9-4a0a-9c75-33dfdf215e4f", "type": "concept", "title": "Clothing and insulation"}, {"id": "2157cb7e-7c6c-417a-94ea-f3d6bdd9ac1e", "type": "concept", "title": "Wind, humidity, and thermal comfort"}]}, {"id": "bd0c386f-2968-468c-944f-8e49b3bc8fcc", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Thermodynamics and human performance", "description": "Students connect the physics of energy transformation to exercise physiology.", "children": [{"id": "b7fd810c-f01f-45f4-a399-c7607848d324", "type": "concept", "title": "First law and energy balance"}, {"id": "4e644fb7-6442-4ed7-bad0-269c68791d82", "type": "concept", "title": "Efficiency and waste heat"}, {"id": "f774c26d-9c9e-4857-ac67-84bf79b8e69c", "type": "concept", "title": "Heat illness and cold exposure applications"}]}]}, {"id": "1d176be3-993b-4130-849a-9c0ac175506b", "type": "topic", "title": "Waves, Sound, Optics, and Biomedical Measurement", "description": "Waves and optics are not only general physics topics; they explain ultrasound, hearing, visual perception, motion capture, imaging, and feedback technologies.", "children": [{"id": "34028373-13d9-44d6-8253-350feb9ed75d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Wave properties and sound", "description": "Students learn the basic variables that describe wave behavior and sound perception.", "children": [{"id": "88e3a2ed-5106-4a58-9275-e4ea43e06dbc", "type": "concept", "title": "Frequency, wavelength, amplitude, and speed"}, {"id": "31fbec7c-9d20-42bd-90bd-ed9bb8b9d5dc", "type": "concept", "title": "Superposition and interference"}, {"id": "f7135141-5d18-47db-938f-1ae5cd0579bf", "type": "concept", "title": "Decibels and hearing safety"}]}, {"id": "af71bb42-c7ac-4696-a007-7dc1b967d6b5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ultrasound and measurement applications", "description": "Students connect wave physics to common health and movement-science technologies.", "children": [{"id": "3b5772e3-a9e3-4096-a9a7-cf5147953f92", "type": "concept", "title": "Reflection, transmission, and attenuation"}, {"id": "46cc00eb-634f-4915-a3ee-5bb1748c2084", "type": "concept", "title": "Doppler principles"}, {"id": "2dff3637-9d00-4aba-9723-a7d9b2486277", "type": "concept", "title": "Muscle, tendon, and blood-flow imaging"}]}, {"id": "10a88479-fade-49b4-ae01-24994e51c6ff", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Geometric optics and visual systems", "description": "Students learn how light forms images and how optical tools support movement analysis.", "children": [{"id": "8225a3de-dece-46f2-b15f-48b769bb9837", "type": "concept", "title": "Reflection and refraction"}, {"id": "3b82e451-ba4c-4b02-accc-388e9310ca03", "type": "concept", "title": "Lenses and image formation"}, {"id": "03027b09-46fb-4e10-aef5-f6fac145cf5a", "type": "concept", "title": "Cameras, motion capture, and visual feedback"}]}]}, {"id": "e77cc210-f0e7-4d22-aade-ba1570df91b6", "type": "topic", "title": "Electricity, Magnetism, and Signals in Kinesiology", "description": "Electrical and magnetic concepts support electrophysiology, instrumentation, rehabilitation technologies, and safe use of devices in labs and clinics.", "children": [{"id": "19de88a9-31ab-4dfa-8d31-7dbc683bd3b9", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Charge, current, voltage, and resistance", "description": "Students learn circuit variables needed to understand sensors and biological signals.", "children": [{"id": "0236390c-bce3-4137-936c-6d0653ca3546", "type": "concept", "title": "Electric charge and electric fields"}, {"id": "c4113d59-850d-495e-ac11-8b93d0ddc9af", "type": "concept", "title": "Current and voltage"}, {"id": "e0aacf8f-2670-4f8b-8134-627dcd080ef7", "type": "concept", "title": "Ohm's law and resistance"}]}, {"id": "5cd0ac86-1a4b-44fb-b5d5-efb82f60e98f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Circuits and measurement devices", "description": "Students connect circuit ideas to practical instrumentation and data collection.", "children": [{"id": "ccb7538e-401a-4f62-be0e-6726bf86a113", "type": "concept", "title": "Series and parallel circuits"}, {"id": "260f6648-b527-4c16-a655-06dfb92c8511", "type": "concept", "title": "Power and energy in circuits"}, {"id": "e4aaadcd-ff79-4777-ad51-fee0ec97fc87", "type": "concept", "title": "Amplifiers, filters, and safety basics"}]}, {"id": "436dc798-fc3c-41dd-81fc-733e3de4a19d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Bioelectric and magnetic applications", "description": "Students see how physics underlies tools used in movement science and health care.", "children": [{"id": "45091854-6d7b-4311-9000-2fd3f12b6e39", "type": "concept", "title": "EMG and ECG signal foundations"}, {"id": "439d2cf7-1ba9-418c-9b85-02612a697023", "type": "concept", "title": "Magnetic fields and induction"}, {"id": "2b69b23e-af72-4164-875a-f92a3a74f23c", "type": "concept", "title": "MRI, stimulation, and device safety concepts"}]}]}, {"id": "8aaad9b2-7e8b-458d-bc2f-fa664a7817b9", "type": "topic", "title": "Modern Physics, Radiation, and Health-Science Literacy", "description": "Modern physics gives kinesiology students enough literacy to understand imaging, radiation safety, quantum-scale claims, and nuclear applications in health contexts.", "children": [{"id": "f5fbbfaf-82f8-45bb-a9e7-8b66599c22b9", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Atomic and nuclear foundations", "description": "Students learn the basic structure of atoms and nuclei without turning the course into advanced physics.", "children": [{"id": "3a3081c2-dadb-425f-9dbb-ff4aadce1edc", "type": "concept", "title": "Atomic energy levels"}, {"id": "c89823d1-e082-4ce9-a7cc-cc05a5a6c27f", "type": "concept", "title": "Isotopes and nuclear stability"}, {"id": "bd447ba0-b711-4137-a14a-ddf8b4be5f37", "type": "concept", "title": "Radioactive decay"}]}, {"id": "73e58f0e-7861-4d7c-8796-a1ba59767a5e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Radiation, dose, and safety", "description": "Students learn to interpret radiation concepts responsibly in clinical and research settings.", "children": [{"id": "1bebedcc-8f36-47b4-98b8-e6fd94fdb748", "type": "concept", "title": "Ionizing vs. nonionizing radiation"}, {"id": "3ace30ae-01d1-40ad-8a8b-e4a9cd76dbd5", "type": "concept", "title": "Half-life and activity"}, {"id": "9332abd7-8898-4bc3-858c-ad67de6da121", "type": "concept", "title": "Dose, shielding, and exposure principles"}]}, {"id": "dec7f2a9-ac84-48ab-a7b2-b258d0dd5498", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Modern imaging and measurement concepts", "description": "Students connect modern physics to technologies they may encounter in health sciences.", "children": [{"id": "f3e9080d-7748-4c00-ae62-40cf7407aeab", "type": "concept", "title": "X-ray and CT basics"}, {"id": "3164ab74-d479-459d-815b-f5f5b64e7a35", "type": "concept", "title": "PET and nuclear medicine concepts"}, {"id": "14d600f2-0eb0-469b-b736-6f082470913e", "type": "concept", "title": "Quantum ideas in sensors and spectroscopy"}]}]}, {"id": "b922fb15-72b2-4076-946c-3ba388a37a9a", "type": "topic", "title": "Integrated Laboratory Methods, Sensors, and Biomechanics Applications", "description": "The final topic turns physics into practice. Students integrate measurement, mechanics, instrumentation, ethics, and communication in movement-analysis problems.", "children": [{"id": "f776f9c0-631d-4b92-90b7-ff2799877ed7", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Data acquisition and sensor literacy", "description": "Students learn how physics measurements become usable movement data.", "children": [{"id": "d6ff11f5-021d-449f-836e-5c7d83d2f6aa", "type": "concept", "title": "Force plates, accelerometers, IMUs, and timing gates"}, {"id": "ce486555-461c-438a-a77c-b993d5a7dac6", "type": "concept", "title": "Sampling frequency and aliasing"}, {"id": "b1cc872f-ff50-4abf-a551-9b0f460fd174", "type": "concept", "title": "Calibration and drift"}]}, {"id": "97d914e1-d3bf-4441-859a-3a5e78e47214", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Integrated movement-analysis casework", "description": "Students synthesize multiple physics ideas in real kinesiology contexts.", "children": [{"id": "cf5f7795-fd26-4416-a632-75581dd526c0", "type": "concept", "title": "Gait, jumping, lifting, throwing, and balance cases"}, {"id": "543b3d58-55db-473e-a982-f6ec3c3fcf9f", "type": "concept", "title": "Linking kinematics, kinetics, energy, and power"}, {"id": "bb9fa5d2-a7a1-448d-96ad-e3dc412cd0b9", "type": "concept", "title": "Interpreting results for performance or rehabilitation"}]}, {"id": "0d5a6e3a-c9e8-4e17-ab55-43fc39640047", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ethics, communication, and reproducibility", "description": "Students practice responsible reporting so physics evidence can support good decisions.", "children": [{"id": "d05390aa-0120-4ec3-8841-666a9a2a6b7e", "type": "concept", "title": "Data privacy and human participants"}, {"id": "1d1af835-98a7-4be0-9f63-f7041a7b42cd", "type": "concept", "title": "Transparent assumptions and uncertainty"}, {"id": "188bb84c-24f5-46ce-b50c-80cbdf052a89", "type": "concept", "title": "Clear visualizations and technical writing"}]}]}], "dbBacked": true}$gpjson$::jsonb,
  true,
  false,
  'course',
  'public'
where not exists (
  select 1 from public.learning_paths where slug = 'general-physics'
);

update public.learning_paths
set
  title = 'General Physics',
  goal = 'General Physics',
  summary = 'This course teaches introductory physics through the lens of human movement, exercise, sport, rehabilitation, and health-science instrumentation. Students develop fluency with mechanics, fluids, thermal physics, waves, optics, electricity, magnetism, and modern physics while repeatedly applying those ideas to bodies, equipment, environments, and data. The goal is not only to solve physics problems, but to reason like a movement scientist: define the system, model the forces and motion, measure carefully, communicate uncertainty, and translate findings into defensible performance or clinical insight.',
  data = $gpjson${"slug": "general-physics", "title": "General Physics", "description": "This course teaches introductory physics through the lens of human movement, exercise, sport, rehabilitation, and health-science instrumentation. Students develop fluency with mechanics, fluids, thermal physics, waves, optics, electricity, magnetism, and modern physics while repeatedly applying those ideas to bodies, equipment, environments, and data. The goal is not only to solve physics problems, but to reason like a movement scientist: define the system, model the forces and motion, measure carefully, communicate uncertainty, and translate findings into defensible performance or clinical insight.", "topics": [{"id": "6946e641-6b99-49e4-b598-d0da264a8b7a", "type": "topic", "title": "Measurement, Units, Modeling, and Quantitative Reasoning for Movement Science", "description": "This opening topic builds the habits that make physics usable in kinesiology: careful measurement, defensible assumptions, dimensional checks, and model-based reasoning about the moving body.", "children": [{"id": "32350b91-2b51-42a7-8be6-f6caf977d8c7", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Measurement systems and dimensional analysis", "description": "Students learn to treat units as a language of physical meaning, catching errors before they become bad conclusions.", "children": [{"id": "4504d833-c113-4936-8352-06ac48ce3b40", "type": "concept", "title": "SI and common clinical/sport units"}, {"id": "2307ebfe-15dc-4014-a7f8-52722771308c", "type": "concept", "title": "Dimensional consistency and unit conversion"}, {"id": "45fee0c2-6484-4c40-8e0f-3269835735c6", "type": "concept", "title": "Order-of-magnitude estimation"}]}, {"id": "45cf3599-79ea-42b7-8293-258af14c0d14", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Modeling the body and equipment", "description": "The body is complex, so students practice choosing simplified models that are useful without pretending they are perfect.", "children": [{"id": "c20d3c22-eaa6-4699-9250-280a62abd2af", "type": "concept", "title": "Particle, rigid-body, and linked-segment models"}, {"id": "e4695ce1-32d5-41b3-bace-f40392772351", "type": "concept", "title": "Assumptions, constraints, and model limitations"}, {"id": "df71aae3-dd6b-43bc-a67b-8f780efe31ca", "type": "concept", "title": "Scaling laws for size, mass, and performance"}]}, {"id": "ed18df45-fca7-4c72-bb04-8de748361a7c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Uncertainty and significant figures", "description": "Physics in kinesiology depends on data quality; this subtopic teaches students to express confidence and limits honestly.", "children": [{"id": "1dfff735-9d89-4fae-846c-e9278d5f3882", "type": "concept", "title": "Random vs. systematic error"}, {"id": "e2ab4c32-c0b5-4256-b94d-23d585e4f731", "type": "concept", "title": "Precision, accuracy, and resolution"}, {"id": "0b1a286e-e2f5-44b2-9dbb-d9d1f7541bed", "type": "concept", "title": "Propagation of uncertainty in calculated results"}]}]}, {"id": "192584b4-89ed-4858-ba6a-4fbc45f69dcd", "type": "topic", "title": "Vectors, Scalars, Free-Body Diagrams, and Coordinate Systems", "description": "Movement science depends on direction as much as magnitude. This topic gives students the visual and mathematical grammar for representing forces, motion, and body orientation.", "children": [{"id": "59f5d40c-1562-4c39-b7e1-50809ed38fa5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Vectors and components", "description": "Students learn to decompose motion and force into usable pieces so complex movement becomes solvable.", "children": [{"id": "db4130ff-aff9-4f16-9961-097b14493e3c", "type": "concept", "title": "Scalar-vector distinction"}, {"id": "5a795337-7a4a-460d-87e6-dae933077be5", "type": "concept", "title": "Cartesian components and resultants"}, {"id": "be805e43-18aa-47c6-9afd-09af7e084ce5", "type": "concept", "title": "Unit vectors and anatomical directions"}]}, {"id": "7ed20368-3773-49ee-accd-657c46165590", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Free-body diagrams", "description": "Free-body diagrams help students identify exactly which interactions matter in a movement or exercise problem.", "children": [{"id": "9cd30a8b-293b-4e6d-b525-b79ecfef1c66", "type": "concept", "title": "Body isolation and external forces"}, {"id": "e35479aa-adf3-4089-ab69-9ec0bd115223", "type": "concept", "title": "Normal, friction, tension, weight, and applied forces"}, {"id": "388c1012-5fd3-4abe-974c-25a8029aebb3", "type": "concept", "title": "Common diagram mistakes in movement settings"}]}, {"id": "7d893be7-7d25-4d27-a55a-10e5baf64d07", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Reference frames and coordinate systems", "description": "Students learn that conclusions depend on the frame of observation, especially in sport, gait, and equipment analysis.", "children": [{"id": "cd8741b4-07be-408c-abcb-3504bdaa7210", "type": "concept", "title": "Laboratory vs. body-centered coordinates"}, {"id": "4b6b3cc9-7c71-4c71-9b75-b1da1292541e", "type": "concept", "title": "Sagittal, frontal, and transverse-plane conventions"}, {"id": "494245a5-1e00-4868-89f1-4de8322ba69b", "type": "concept", "title": "Relative and absolute motion"}]}]}, {"id": "d792dfa1-42a1-4adf-99ce-7ad838155212", "type": "topic", "title": "Linear Kinematics of Human Motion", "description": "This topic analyzes motion without first asking why it happens. It prepares students to describe gait, jumping, throwing, and rehabilitation tasks using displacement, velocity, and acceleration.", "children": [{"id": "a4cca952-c334-4481-8228-d46e2b4068d8", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Position, displacement, velocity, and acceleration", "description": "Students learn how to translate observed motion into quantitative descriptions.", "children": [{"id": "5cadb21e-9c8f-446f-835f-71a8f9719a25", "type": "concept", "title": "One- and two-dimensional motion"}, {"id": "d260bb2d-28c1-4ed8-ac0b-8d22820968ed", "type": "concept", "title": "Average vs. instantaneous quantities"}, {"id": "7acb0d02-f8c2-43a2-a98d-423584bb663c", "type": "concept", "title": "Graphs of position, velocity, and acceleration"}]}, {"id": "0fb4db4b-a349-4150-a0e5-297d3e2708cc", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Projectile motion in sport and exercise", "description": "Projectile motion links physics to jumping, throwing, striking, and landing tasks.", "children": [{"id": "be78321b-eee0-49dd-8618-000122e85201", "type": "concept", "title": "Launch angle and launch speed"}, {"id": "10d3761c-0d64-4604-b19d-1e6980f00d73", "type": "concept", "title": "Trajectory and range"}, {"id": "83ab5a20-ec4b-417d-aaea-7d02f3b765c4", "type": "concept", "title": "Air resistance as a modeling limitation"}]}, {"id": "e7a96dac-7464-46b5-a623-dc365bbb0255", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Video-based movement analysis", "description": "Students connect physics equations to practical tools used in coaching, clinical assessment, and research labs.", "children": [{"id": "a9e04d46-c97d-4632-8a10-ae9bb8fbe664", "type": "concept", "title": "Frame rate and sampling"}, {"id": "4b042f6a-46d4-4965-a8b5-0b71c93f92cf", "type": "concept", "title": "Digitizing landmarks"}, {"id": "ad5bdec5-4665-468a-91df-c473b860b065", "type": "concept", "title": "Calculating velocity and acceleration from position data"}]}]}, {"id": "555bd80f-2056-424b-b27a-9db277b88b1a", "type": "topic", "title": "Newton's Laws, Forces, and Interaction Models", "description": "Dynamics explains why motion changes. Students use Newton's laws to connect external loads, ground reaction forces, friction, and body acceleration.", "children": [{"id": "84ddf680-800c-4401-a7e9-03211a4d8a62", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Newton's three laws in movement contexts", "description": "Students learn to interpret movement as the result of interactions rather than isolated effort.", "children": [{"id": "3c7515ca-efa7-4b1e-98fe-d10c70aa2b2f", "type": "concept", "title": "Inertia and motion state"}, {"id": "b64e69a1-2f28-4143-bca2-236e39f5f41d", "type": "concept", "title": "Net force and acceleration"}, {"id": "8f504dfa-cc96-4d0f-aa26-2540f83b4681", "type": "concept", "title": "Action-reaction pairs"}]}, {"id": "88b3c142-65f0-4108-9652-b4f90a433feb", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Common forces in kinesiology", "description": "Students identify forces that shape human performance and safety in exercise environments.", "children": [{"id": "19cd5153-a520-45ef-b466-06fb2cec4ce0", "type": "concept", "title": "Weight, normal force, friction, tension, and drag"}, {"id": "783bc713-08c0-4a0a-b16c-ff4d1c7caddf", "type": "concept", "title": "Ground reaction forces"}, {"id": "e1d0d728-b136-4c5e-bd82-4da8e7e853a8", "type": "concept", "title": "Contact forces in equipment and surfaces"}]}, {"id": "d2843e90-b878-4d9c-889d-3eb9dd405b57", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Friction, traction, and stability", "description": "Friction determines whether the body can accelerate, stop, turn, or maintain balance.", "children": [{"id": "89f2a397-2471-4ef1-813d-65b14eec5b64", "type": "concept", "title": "Static and kinetic friction"}, {"id": "6d680119-f590-464d-8f67-26554b3f3eaa", "type": "concept", "title": "Coefficient of friction"}, {"id": "0a86358b-7a5d-486e-bb15-952909a7b9dc", "type": "concept", "title": "Footwear, flooring, and injury prevention"}]}]}, {"id": "bdfa3415-471d-4b0e-aa63-5a1398276971", "type": "topic", "title": "Work, Energy, Power, and Metabolic Connections", "description": "Energy methods provide a powerful way to analyze movement, exercise output, and mechanical efficiency without solving every force at every instant.", "children": [{"id": "551fec33-3c0c-40fa-8727-204e24ef57dd", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Mechanical work and kinetic energy", "description": "Students learn how force over distance changes the body's motion.", "children": [{"id": "8c9c1c47-a68c-45af-9ba1-e7db7289fcbe", "type": "concept", "title": "Work by constant and variable forces"}, {"id": "cf0a546c-7794-44b7-a19f-a67ecd80bae0", "type": "concept", "title": "Kinetic energy and the work-energy theorem"}, {"id": "19131f9a-c95f-41ea-97e8-6f32dbe6053f", "type": "concept", "title": "Positive and negative work in movement"}]}, {"id": "32cd04a2-07de-4020-8868-f60e097fa9da", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Potential energy and energy conservation", "description": "Students connect body position, storage, release, and transformation of energy.", "children": [{"id": "af0cd57a-c629-482d-a7d5-31f311f89ece", "type": "concept", "title": "Gravitational potential energy"}, {"id": "47989d05-1dac-46e4-9112-a87570e82829", "type": "concept", "title": "Elastic energy in tendons and equipment"}, {"id": "04f10ef7-aa34-49e7-961f-281718175e81", "type": "concept", "title": "Conservative and nonconservative forces"}]}, {"id": "6a18ab95-b340-4b2a-b29a-be425375a22d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Power, efficiency, and human performance", "description": "Students connect physics to exercise physiology through the rate of energy transfer.", "children": [{"id": "ebf421a6-4b39-4ce7-a455-abe717e797f8", "type": "concept", "title": "Mechanical power output"}, {"id": "f97a0a67-54e8-4a18-9dbc-de15ee4a4886", "type": "concept", "title": "Efficiency and energy loss"}, {"id": "3943f6de-9098-452f-a7ab-f2fb882fa9d5", "type": "concept", "title": "Cycling, running, lifting, and jumping examples"}]}]}, {"id": "fb93ed43-3675-470b-912e-3fecaf3814d7", "type": "topic", "title": "Momentum, Impulse, Collisions, and Impact Safety", "description": "Momentum and impulse help students analyze rapid force events such as landing, cutting, collisions, falls, and sport impacts.", "children": [{"id": "92551632-0295-4384-ae68-73ec8cadb92a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Linear momentum and conservation", "description": "Students learn when momentum is conserved and how it constrains motion before and after interactions.", "children": [{"id": "a02dd5a4-8bfe-47d9-bc6a-b1aa3b3f4c64", "type": "concept", "title": "Momentum as mass times velocity"}, {"id": "20e1f828-925f-46d1-a336-f494e1269565", "type": "concept", "title": "System boundaries"}, {"id": "13d99e44-a13c-4cb2-8b5a-97501a30b32b", "type": "concept", "title": "Conservation in low-external-force intervals"}]}, {"id": "b78bfe27-00e7-4b48-b1de-f8503d299685", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Impulse and force-time relationships", "description": "Impulse shows why both force magnitude and contact time matter for performance and safety.", "children": [{"id": "0bc0f6c8-ba68-411a-b677-f41bea88bbb2", "type": "concept", "title": "Impulse-momentum theorem"}, {"id": "aaddb78a-f9b7-4953-a5d5-fbf58f905c44", "type": "concept", "title": "Force-time curves"}, {"id": "4ed85b1e-a3fb-4e43-b9c1-5e4258c72474", "type": "concept", "title": "Landing technique and load attenuation"}]}, {"id": "0f89939d-dd99-465e-928e-f7699dfbe703", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Collisions and impact mechanics", "description": "Students distinguish productive impact from dangerous loading by examining energy transfer and deformation.", "children": [{"id": "fa7108e4-02a6-4ac8-9a3b-9c93082474fd", "type": "concept", "title": "Elastic and inelastic collisions"}, {"id": "5119ac21-4c63-4c12-bb2c-cdda3357c8f3", "type": "concept", "title": "Coefficient of restitution"}, {"id": "e06e7625-7d92-44d9-8966-67a97d286ac5", "type": "concept", "title": "Helmets, mats, footwear, and protective design"}]}]}, {"id": "f6c2a304-d74e-47ca-b32d-dee7b12bba98", "type": "topic", "title": "Torque, Rotational Motion, and Angular Momentum", "description": "Most human movement is rotational around joints. This topic gives students the tools to analyze turning, swinging, joint moments, and angular control.", "children": [{"id": "2a033e9f-ddaa-4b57-a1f0-6c17bca37e38", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Angular kinematics", "description": "Students learn rotational analogs of linear motion so they can describe joint and segment motion.", "children": [{"id": "946d01e4-dcc3-4827-9529-184ecba7cdf0", "type": "concept", "title": "Angular displacement, velocity, and acceleration"}, {"id": "d21dca3f-99e6-4a57-bbff-2e55f279fea4", "type": "concept", "title": "Radians and degrees"}, {"id": "f1c7d2f4-8e2a-4ffe-a604-c4eadb7fdbe1", "type": "concept", "title": "Relationship between linear and angular quantities"}]}, {"id": "f6af1da6-b0a0-4380-8937-e33839c0fc67", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Torque and moment arms", "description": "Torque explains how forces create rotation at joints and equipment pivots.", "children": [{"id": "01b7b836-398d-44f9-aea9-dc1ec765d7e7", "type": "concept", "title": "Torque as force times moment arm"}, {"id": "4c47f969-ebb8-4f04-888c-2e0fe21855e5", "type": "concept", "title": "Internal and external moments"}, {"id": "39b37997-f454-46fa-a9ff-041ad5848445", "type": "concept", "title": "Joint torque examples"}]}, {"id": "080d43ad-5e56-479b-9613-3ff15154830c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Moment of inertia and angular momentum", "description": "Students learn why mass distribution changes rotational difficulty and control.", "children": [{"id": "eacbb222-77be-441f-8763-40b097faea29", "type": "concept", "title": "Moment of inertia"}, {"id": "eb62cfc1-98f2-42ad-af14-b01d27847e28", "type": "concept", "title": "Angular impulse and angular momentum"}, {"id": "c25b2426-33d4-46d8-b7de-4abf81b17276", "type": "concept", "title": "Spin, swing, and aerial movement applications"}]}]}, {"id": "3b889fee-f59c-49a7-b249-b47f0b06aa3e", "type": "topic", "title": "Static Equilibrium, Center of Mass, and Human Balance", "description": "Balance is a physics problem with clinical and performance consequences. Students analyze when bodies and objects remain stable or tip, rotate, or fall.", "children": [{"id": "622b8aa2-3be2-4ff5-8590-fa12b8e72c84", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Conditions for equilibrium", "description": "Students learn that stillness requires both zero net force and zero net torque.", "children": [{"id": "1edbaa0e-3680-4f27-bdb8-ea443b311b6d", "type": "concept", "title": "Translational equilibrium"}, {"id": "d85e786b-7c5a-44ae-bc9f-fbf2f20fa48c", "type": "concept", "title": "Rotational equilibrium"}, {"id": "ef798b9e-5c45-4cce-9147-1473540bee51", "type": "concept", "title": "Solving multi-force equilibrium problems"}]}, {"id": "5fb83843-dcf1-4dbb-bc78-662f7cff0356", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Center of mass and base of support", "description": "Students connect body posture to stability, fall risk, and sport skill execution.", "children": [{"id": "7d4434a3-adb0-480c-abd6-d302117dd70c", "type": "concept", "title": "Center of mass location"}, {"id": "42afc347-25c2-4c22-94bb-77ef5b825121", "type": "concept", "title": "Line of gravity"}, {"id": "3f9d154b-ef0e-4aa9-b02d-949615686071", "type": "concept", "title": "Base of support and stability limits"}]}, {"id": "b6f3d1ab-e34a-472d-a6b9-a6121eb13216", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Balance assessment and applications", "description": "Students apply equilibrium to real movement settings rather than treating it as abstract statics.", "children": [{"id": "6312693a-80e0-4de7-a21a-b348765dcb04", "type": "concept", "title": "Single-leg stance and postural sway"}, {"id": "7659ead7-18a1-4253-a6da-658033278b04", "type": "concept", "title": "Load carriage and assistive devices"}, {"id": "c001c70d-4481-4a6c-93bf-06516c6cea70", "type": "concept", "title": "Older adult and rehabilitation examples"}]}]}, {"id": "dcae89e4-ad88-4e79-855f-2812058f863a", "type": "topic", "title": "Levers, Mechanical Advantage, and Musculoskeletal Applications", "description": "The musculoskeletal system acts through lever arrangements. Students learn how bones, joints, muscles, and external loads create mechanical advantages and tradeoffs.", "children": [{"id": "a4a9a3c7-a54b-46d0-b64e-a00d7f181cc5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Classes of levers in the body", "description": "Students identify how body structures transform muscle force into movement or stability.", "children": [{"id": "71208d84-4a29-4b36-97c6-84afd4373127", "type": "concept", "title": "First-, second-, and third-class levers"}, {"id": "4413e961-f08d-4bee-a871-e20db5f150a2", "type": "concept", "title": "Fulcrum, effort, and load"}, {"id": "f23fbb97-d88c-47ba-9299-adc57cb5712d", "type": "concept", "title": "Examples at neck, ankle, elbow, and knee"}]}, {"id": "340b65c2-bb68-42f2-9b92-580aae50c40f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Mechanical advantage and tradeoffs", "description": "Students learn why the body often favors speed and range of motion over force advantage.", "children": [{"id": "c5d0cd9f-4515-47d1-ac21-4129eb1b2395", "type": "concept", "title": "Mechanical advantage ratio"}, {"id": "ae5af7d5-06de-43b3-9583-325caa028a3e", "type": "concept", "title": "Force-speed-distance tradeoffs"}, {"id": "42d07351-8192-4ab0-8431-26ed83f25431", "type": "concept", "title": "Exercise technique implications"}]}, {"id": "7958b5fb-9898-4c6c-bf26-2aa1257754bf", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Joint loading and resistance exercise", "description": "Students connect lever mechanics to training decisions and injury-aware programming.", "children": [{"id": "ced01c42-4c14-4ea5-aba6-d26118bff731", "type": "concept", "title": "External moment arms in exercises"}, {"id": "98c31d93-8e0c-4ef0-bb65-82bd2319891d", "type": "concept", "title": "Load placement and joint stress"}, {"id": "0bfc4207-ee26-4a04-9cc5-d95e1bc29318", "type": "concept", "title": "Machines, free weights, and bands"}]}]}, {"id": "e8534bbe-f05f-4616-a65d-d265dd67a246", "type": "topic", "title": "Elasticity, Stress, Strain, and Tissue/Equipment Mechanics", "description": "Movement depends on materials: bone, tendon, muscle, footwear, mats, and equipment all deform under load. This topic introduces the physics of deformation and injury mechanisms.", "children": [{"id": "3a74ca8d-492d-4cd0-a7de-83aa50c2ce8d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Stress, strain, and elastic behavior", "description": "Students learn how internal loading is quantified and why deformation matters.", "children": [{"id": "28661205-7923-47af-9c20-0348526aef49", "type": "concept", "title": "Normal and shear stress"}, {"id": "50a1c04a-a697-40d1-bd07-0088d922cc6c", "type": "concept", "title": "Strain and deformation"}, {"id": "469f9696-d5db-44e5-9295-e3f6b9975cfb", "type": "concept", "title": "Young's modulus and stiffness"}]}, {"id": "6c40073d-657b-41f4-8221-337d4ae29ebb", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Biological tissue mechanics", "description": "Students connect material properties to performance, adaptation, and injury risk.", "children": [{"id": "6d2b2fe0-2f99-4e79-9c98-19584461b8be", "type": "concept", "title": "Bone, tendon, ligament, and muscle properties"}, {"id": "fa39b111-a593-4ffc-b312-a76a15f3ac60", "type": "concept", "title": "Viscoelasticity and hysteresis"}, {"id": "dc08942c-cc5e-4df2-9a80-f1c2125147f4", "type": "concept", "title": "Creep, relaxation, and fatigue"}]}, {"id": "a5dc9ad8-6d7f-4899-bdf2-9ea2692db781", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Equipment and surface mechanics", "description": "Students evaluate how materials mediate force transfer between person and environment.", "children": [{"id": "6bc85807-05a1-43e0-9ff8-8baee026e876", "type": "concept", "title": "Shoe cushioning and stiffness"}, {"id": "63dfa154-f842-4f0a-9e08-b0caed17905e", "type": "concept", "title": "Mats and impact attenuation"}, {"id": "55b6dece-aa21-4768-9c7b-5cc68abd2492", "type": "concept", "title": "Prosthetic and orthotic materials"}]}]}, {"id": "875e8d7e-8fde-4768-97c6-b7e9b2d46248", "type": "topic", "title": "Fluids, Pressure, Buoyancy, and Cardiopulmonary Applications", "description": "Fluid physics connects swimming, blood flow, breathing, pressure, buoyancy, and exercise in water or air.", "children": [{"id": "b3091e52-d21b-4f17-bdd1-35ff9bbd3f70", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Pressure and hydrostatics", "description": "Students learn how fluid pressure varies with depth and affects bodies in water and circulation.", "children": [{"id": "c9c24747-ca4f-4524-acad-21572bc37425", "type": "concept", "title": "Pressure definition and units"}, {"id": "656bb696-0a18-4fd6-94a3-ce6cd9e08cf9", "type": "concept", "title": "Pascal's principle"}, {"id": "44a22d41-785d-47df-a50d-0a4a0e1b9b26", "type": "concept", "title": "Hydrostatic pressure in aquatic exercise"}]}, {"id": "aeaaef01-9c6e-44c9-8b4d-92a5d6ca6aca", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Buoyancy and flotation", "description": "Students connect Archimedes' principle to swimming, rehabilitation, and body composition contexts.", "children": [{"id": "dccb0c7f-01b2-4053-b94f-865da08e93bc", "type": "concept", "title": "Buoyant force"}, {"id": "6506d65b-ea7b-43a5-b671-abcb19b0241e", "type": "concept", "title": "Apparent weight in water"}, {"id": "4ffb7bf1-c189-4cd3-9d66-299cdfe49bf0", "type": "concept", "title": "Center of buoyancy and stability"}]}, {"id": "d1f83814-36c6-42e5-a4a3-e788cc05ac88", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Fluid flow, drag, and biological transport", "description": "Students study how flow resistance shapes swimming, cycling, blood flow, and breathing.", "children": [{"id": "25359366-334a-44b0-9b66-9ce8e00df6dc", "type": "concept", "title": "Continuity and flow rate"}, {"id": "6422d192-7d04-4ded-8842-a78aced7c30a", "type": "concept", "title": "Laminar vs. turbulent flow"}, {"id": "449cbec4-6f1e-4d77-96d1-92d74abebda1", "type": "concept", "title": "Drag force and streamlining"}]}]}, {"id": "fdf2db2a-ba07-4f00-ab82-3454f986a609", "type": "topic", "title": "Heat, Thermodynamics, and Exercise Environments", "description": "Thermal physics helps students understand body temperature regulation, environmental stress, heat transfer, and energy transformation during exercise.", "children": [{"id": "9d90da77-ce94-444e-b987-e61f54590a82", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Temperature, heat, and internal energy", "description": "Students distinguish thermal concepts that are often confused in everyday language.", "children": [{"id": "3be23d9c-2f31-4e82-bfe2-d8d646d1a247", "type": "concept", "title": "Temperature vs. heat"}, {"id": "ff562ba0-b622-4ffc-9230-f497ad5014e5", "type": "concept", "title": "Spe